A||N||3||Seanh||Finally building my new V8...which one?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||11-10-2001||12:15 PM||||Ok, I am getting a very nice insureance settlement from a drunk drive who hit my fiero. I'm going to do the work myself and make a little money to get me started. I plan on buying Archies $1500 V8 install kit. Now, I'm haveing some trouble deciding on an engine I want. I would really love an LT1/4 with the Corvette intake just for the looks of seeing it under the hood of Fiero. First off I am definatly going with fuel injection...If not LT1/4 injection system, then TPI. I am deciding between a 350, 383, 377, or 400. If I do 350, I'm just going to use a 87+ roller block, with aluminum heads and either TPI or vette LT intake system. I'm not sure what I want yet though...<P>First off, can I use an 87+ 350 block w/LT heads and achieve reverse flow cooling? I really want reverse flow cooling, its a major reason I want the LT engine so badly. If I can't get reverse flow with a normal block, then I'm definatly going with an LT block.<P>Then heads. If I don't use TPI, then I want the vette intake on my engine. So I was thinking I'd use some ported and polished up LT4 heads. <P>Also, the differences in each engine? I'v only had a 350, never owned any of the others. I want a great 1/4 car with enough power to beat others off the line around town, and would like the power in the upper end, but not high upper end. I never take the car over 120, so I need to choose an apprioate power band here. Cam is still up grabs, absoultly no idea what cam I will look at until I figure out which engine I'm doing. I like one that wraps fast, and will give me great 1/4 mile times, I'm hopeing to get into atleast the 12's if not faster...I want to predict higher times so I'm happier when I do better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> . <P>Anyway, what do you think I should do, and how would these ideas turn out?<P>BTW, the 383 would be in an LT block w/383 stroker crank.<P>One other thing, if anyone out there has Desktop Dyno/Drag Strip 2000 on their computer, would someone be willing to give me a copy of that program? That would be so helpful in this stage, but I don't feel like ordering it and waiting for it to get here.<P>Going to stick with an auto tranny here too, prolly the TH125c beefed up, or the 440t4. Which would run the 1/4 faster?||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||11-10-2001||12:40 PM||||4 speed auto will beat a 3 but may not live as long.<BR>400 chevys don't live long bad motor.<BR>you want more lower end power in a auto so do not go tooo wild on cams and valves/porting.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||11-10-2001||03:36 PM||||Well, there are numerous changes for the reverse flow cooling engines.  Either get a conventional TPI V8, or get an LT1/LT4.  Don't try to convert one into the other.  The LT1/4 requires moving the engine to the left some to clear the hardware on the front of the engine - Archie's regular V8 kit doesn't address this, I don't think.  Archie is the man to ask about that.  It can be done, but it's more involved than a regular TPI V8 swap.  If the LT1 style intake will fit a TPI era engine, then I'd do that route.  If not, I'd stick with TPI.  I like the idea of an LT4 in a Fiero - but only if I found a complete LT4 to swap in.  I wouldn't try to convert or build one.  <P>Archie can answer all your questions there.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||11-10-2001||04:02 PM||||I'v talked to archie a while back about this and as I recall he said the only thing I'd need in addition to the kit will be some custom length axel's. I would really like to have reverse flow cooling, but I'll look into putting the LT1 vette intake on a regular 87+ roller block. ||||reg||1
